{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-53472",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14165760/assets/277e019b26f72cffd7647abe694338acd099e4d48fc532a7e9d6ad362a49dcc9.png)\n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the shorter leg has length $x=6$ and the longer leg has length $\\sqrt{3}x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{AC}=\\sqrt{3}x=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 6=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the longer leg is $6\\sqrt{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
